14. Conclusion
Ü cannot differentiate between fragrances, you cannot
be a perfume maker; if É no legs, you cannot be a
runner. If ß tone-deaf, you cannot be a musician.
To become ¿wright you should be a man with im-
agination and common sense, to begin with. You Ä
observant. You must never be satisfied with superficial knowl-
edge. You ư patience to search for causes. You must
ã sense of balance and good taste. You should know
economy, psychology, physiology, sociology. You can learn
these things with patience and hard work—and ğ ţ
learn them, no approach will make é playwright of you.
We are often astonished at how glibly people decide to be
writers or playwrights. It takes about three years of appren-
ticeship ś é shoemaker; ³ is true of carpen-
try or any other skill. Why should playwriting—one ¡
hardest professions £ world—be acquired overnight,
without serious study? The dialectical approach will help
those who have prepared for this work. It will also help the
beginner by giving him a clear picture ¡ obstacles ½
path and ¡ road he must travel if he is to achieve his
ambitions.
